/*
Time Complexity: Exponential in the worst case.

The recursion explores an exponential number of subsets (O(2ⁿ) in the worst case). 
Additionally, for each valid combination found, a copy of the current list is made, 
which costs O(n) in the worst case. Therefore, the overall time complexity is exponential.
O(n · 2ⁿ) in the worst case

Space Complexity: O(n) auxiliary space for recursion and current path, plus O(2ⁿ) output space 
for storing all valid combinations.

INTUITION

Sort first so that duplicates sit next to each other.

At each index, you have two choices:

Pick the current number and move forward.

Skip the current number.

If you skip, you must skip all identical values, otherwise you’ll recreate the same combination.

The while loop ensures that after returning from the pick path, you jump past all duplicates before exploring the skip path.

This guarantees:

Every number is used at most once.

Duplicate combinations are never formed
*/


class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> curSet = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(candidates,0,target,curSet,res);
        return res;
    }
    public void dfs(int[] a,int i,int target,List<Integer> curSet,List<List<Integer>> res){
        if(target == 0){
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(curSet));
            return;
        }
        if(target < 0 || i>=a.length || target-a[i]<0){
            return;
        }
       
        curSet.add(a[i]);
        dfs(a,i+1,target-a[i],curSet,res);
        curSet.remove(curSet.size()-1);
        // if a[i]==a[i+1] dont choose the same number again after going on that path and returning from the above line
        while(i<a.length-1 && a[i]==a[i+1])i++;
        dfs(a,i+1,target,curSet,res);
        
    }
}
